  "summary": "WIRED reverse-engineered Flock Safety's browser code to reveal how its AI-powered search tools work, including a FreeForm natural-language person search, a Smart Sort ranking system, and an AI watch list that continuously scans cameras for people matching written descriptions. The investigation found Flock's content-moderation system blocks some categories (race, religion, nationality) but only warns on political/cultural expression, and that guardrails like case-code requirements and Audit Assistance are off by default. Experts say the safeguards log misuse but don't prevent it, while officer stalking cases using Flock continue to mount.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Flock's guardrails may discourage some abusive uses of its tools, and will record them, but will not stop them.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "At least 50 officers in the US have recently been charged with or accused of misusing license plate readers, according to The Washington Post. Forty-six of those cases involve Flock.",
      "paragraph_idx": 29
    },
    {
      "quote": "In a search last year, an officer in California typed \"American flag.\" The search drew a block when aimed at a person, then ran across 11,000 cameras when aimed at vehicles instead.",
      "paragraph_idx": 41
    },
    {
      "quote": "The filtering, he says, is \"a speck on the back of a giant surveillance machine\" whose database can be probed by AI for \"your-imagination-is-the-limit fishing expeditions.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 45
    },
    {
      "quote": "A South Carolina sheriff's office turned Audit Assistance on earlier this month, and an internal-affairs officer identified more than 2,700 allegedly unauthorized searches the next day.",
      "paragraph_idx": 49
    }
  ],
